@page "/wasm"

<PageTitle>Standalone WebAssembly · Belemetry</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Standalone WebAssembly</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        A standalone Blazor WebAssembly app has no server that renders its head, so there is no
        <code>&lt;BelemetryScript /&gt;</code>: one registration in <code>Program.cs</code> and
        <code>&lt;BelemetryInitializer /&gt;</code> in the root component are the whole setup. Both halves -
        the browser script and the .NET pipeline - run in the reader's browser.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Register in Program.cs</h3>
    <p>
        The host kind is detected: no ASP.NET Core hosting in the container and a browser underneath means
        WebAssembly, so the .NET pipeline runs here and exports with the browser's own <code>fetch</code>.
        The same exporters are handed to the browser script, unless an exporter sets
        <code>IncludeInBrowser = false</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@ProgramCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> The initializer in App.razor</h3>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;BelemetryInitializer /&gt;</code> imports the script, starts it with the configuration from
        <code>Program.cs</code>, and connects it to .NET: consent and the session are shared, and the
        unload flush is wired up. Place it once, next to the router.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@AppRazorCode" Language="Razor" Expanded="true" />
    <div class="bb-note bb-note-warn">
        <p>
            Add <code>@@using Bit.Belemetry</code> to <code>_Imports.razor</code>. Without it
            <code>&lt;BelemetryInitializer /&gt;</code> compiles as an unknown HTML element - a warning, not an error -
            and telemetry silently never starts.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> The optional script tag</h3>
    <p>
        Adding the module to <code>index.html</code> makes the browser download it in parallel with the
        .NET runtime instead of after it, and puts <code>window.BitBelemetry</code> on the page before Blazor
        starts. The script itself starts when <code>&lt;BelemetryInitializer /&gt;</code> hands it the
        configuration.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@IndexHtmlCode" Language="HTML" Expanded="true" />
    <div class="bb-note">
        <p>
            Starting late costs less than it sounds: web vitals, the document-load span, resource timing and
            long tasks are read from the browser's buffered performance entries, so the script sees them
            whenever it starts. What it cannot see is a JavaScript error thrown before it started.
        </p>
    </div>
    <p>
        To catch those too, start the script on load the way <code>&lt;BelemetryScript /&gt;</code> does in a Blazor
        Web App: write the browser configuration into <code>index.html</code> yourself, as a
        <code>&lt;script type="application/json" id="bit-belemetry-config"&gt;</code> element before the module (its
        shape is on the <a href="/semantic-conventions">Semantic conventions</a> page). That element then fixes the
        exporters; the later <code>start</code> from <code>&lt;BelemetryInitializer /&gt;</code> is a second start, which
        only updates the consent defaults and the transport, and connects .NET.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@ConfigElementCode" Language="HTML" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Endpoints and CORS</h3>
    <p>
        Everything is exported from the page's origin to the collector's, so the receiver has to allow the
        app's origin. For the OpenTelemetry Collector that is <code>cors.allowed_origins</code> on the OTLP
        HTTP receiver; for the .NET Aspire dashboard, <code>DASHBOARD__OTLP__CORS__ALLOWEDORIGINS</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@CollectorCode" Language="YAML" />
    <div class="bb-note bb-note-warn">
        <p>
            A WebAssembly app's configuration is downloaded by every visitor, so exporter headers - API keys
            included, <code>Headers</code> and <code>BrowserHeaders</code> alike - are public. For a vendor that needs
            a key, put a collector in between that adds it.
        </p>
    </div>
    <p>
        The .NET pipeline exports with the browser's <code>fetch</code> too, so an OTLP exporter set to
        <code>BelemetryOtlpProtocol.Grpc</code> cannot work here as it is: it is turned into OTLP/HTTP to its
        <code>BrowserEndpoint</code> (which must be absolute), and one without a <code>BrowserEndpoint</code> is skipped
        with a startup warning.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> What runs where</h3>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Browser instrumentation</td><td>The script: page views, clicks, web vitals, errors, fetch and XHR spans</td></tr>
                <tr><td>.NET logs, traces, metrics</td><td>In the browser, exported every <code>Batch.FlushInterval</code> and <code>Metrics.ExportInterval</code>. Metrics rely on <code>System.Diagnostics.Metrics</code>, which the WebAssembly SDK turns off by default and the package turns back on (<code>MetricsSupport</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Unload</td><td>On <code>pagehide</code> or when the tab is hidden, the script asks .NET for its pending records already serialized, and sends them with <code>fetch(..., { keepalive: true })</code> - async .NET work does not survive the page going away</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Offline persistence</td><td><code>localStorage</code>, up to <code>Persistence.BrowserMaxBytes</code> (1 MB)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Consent</td><td>App-wide: one decision governs the script and the .NET pipeline alike</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Error floods</td><td>Both the script and the .NET pipeline apply <code>options.Errors</code>: an identical error within <code>DuplicateWindow</code> (5 s) is recorded once, and at most <code>MaxPerMinute</code> (100) errors a minute leave the tab</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_*</code> variables</td><td>Ignored - there is no process environment in the browser</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        Calls made with .NET's <code>HttpClient</code> are recorded once either way. The WebAssembly SDK turns
        <code>HttpClient</code>'s own tracing off (<code>HttpActivityPropagationSupport</code>), so by default its
        requests go out through the browser's <code>fetch</code> and the script records them as fetch spans, adding
        a <code>traceparent</code> only where <code>Browser.PropagateTraceContextTo</code> allows. Set the switch to
        <code>true</code> in the project and .NET traces them instead, as <code>System.Net.Http</code> spans inside
        the current activity's trace; they then carry a <code>traceparent</code>, and the script leaves any request
        that has one alone. The price: <code>HttpClient</code> then sends <code>traceparent</code> to every origin it
        calls, and a cross-origin API that does not allow the header in CORS fails.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@HttpTracingCode" Language="XML" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> API</h3>
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetryInitializer" />
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetryOtlpExporterOptions" />
</div>

@code {
    private const string ProgramCode = """
        using Bit.Belemetry;
        using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Hosting;

        var builder = WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault(args);
        builder.RootComponents.Add<App>("#app");

        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            options.ServiceName = "MyApp";
            options.Environment = builder.HostEnvironment.Environment;

            options.AddOtlpExporter(o =>
            {
                o.Endpoint = new Uri("https://otel.example.com");
                o.Protocol = BelemetryOtlpProtocol.HttpProtobuf;
            });

            options.Traces.Sources.Add("MyApp*");
            options.Metrics.Meters.Add("MyApp");
        });

        await builder.Build().RunAsync();
        """;

    private const string AppRazorCode = """
        @* App.razor *@
        <Router AppAssembly="typeof(App).Assembly">
            <Found Context="routeData">
                <RouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(MainLayout)" />
            </Found>
        </Router>

        <BelemetryInitializer />
        """;

    private const string IndexHtmlCode = """
        <!-- wwwroot/index.html -->
        <head>
            ...
            <script type="module" src="_content/Bit.Belemetry/bit-belemetry.js"></script>
        </head>
        """;

    private const string ConfigElementCode = """
        <!-- wwwroot/index.html - the script starts as soon as the module loads, before Blazor -->
        <head>
            ...
            <script type="application/json" id="bit-belemetry-config">
                { "service": { "name": "MyApp" },
                  "exporters": [ { "type": "otlp", "endpoint": "https://otel.example.com" } ] }
            </script>
            <script type="module" src="_content/Bit.Belemetry/bit-belemetry.js"></script>
        </head>
        """;

    private const string HttpTracingCode = """
        <!-- Client.csproj: let .NET trace HttpClient itself (off in WebAssembly by default) -->
        <PropertyGroup>
            <HttpActivityPropagationSupport>true</HttpActivityPropagationSupport>
        </PropertyGroup>
        """;

    private const string CollectorCode = """
        receivers:
          otlp:
            protocols:
              http:
                endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4318
                cors:
                  allowed_origins: ["https://myapp.example.com"]
                  allowed_headers: ["*"]
        """;
}
